# Schedule AI Voice - example environment file.
# Copy to `.env` and fill in locally. NEVER commit a real .env or a real secret.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma datasource URL. This mission uses a file-based SQLite database so a
# fresh clone needs no external credentials and no network access.
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM provider
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenAI API key used by the agent layer's LlmProvider adapter.
# Leave EMPTY here. Set it only in your local, git-ignored .env file.
# The foundation, scheduling and test suites never require this to be set:
# every test runs against deterministic doubles.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local LLM (Ollama) - Mission 2, the local brain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which LlmProvider a composed runtime should use: scripted | local | openai.
#
# DEFAULTS TO `scripted`, and leaving it that way is the safe choice: the
# scripted provider talks to nobody and spends nothing. Setting this to `local`
# states an INTENT - it does not by itself make anything dial a model, because
# `buildAgentRuntime` still requires a caller to pass `llmProviderConfig`
# explicitly. That is deliberate; see src/app/composition.ts.
LLM_PROVIDER=scripted

# Base URL of the Ollama server. NOTE: inside a container, `localhost` is the
# container - use host.docker.internal to reach the host's Ollama. This is the
# single most common misconfiguration. Run `npm run llm:probe` to check.
LOCAL_LLM_BASE_URL="http://host.docker.internal:11434"

# The Ollama model tag to use. Must already be present on the host; pull it with
#   curl http://<host>:11434/api/pull -d '{"model":"qwen2.5:7b-instruct"}'
# `npm run llm:probe` lists what is present and flags an absent one.
#
# RECOMMENDED AS THE INTENDED DEFAULT, PENDING FOUNDER APPROVAL - and the value
# below is ALREADY that model, so nothing here was changed. The fair five-model
# benchmark (all five candidates re-run one at a time under recorded host
# conditions at num_ctx 16384) recommends qwen2.5:7b-instruct, which is what was
# already configured. What changed is its STATUS: it used to be the default
# because the provider slice needed A model to develop against, and it is now the
# default the evidence earns.
#
# SCOPE: recommended for ENGLISH-language customer-facing use ONLY. NO candidate
# is earned for HEBREW customer-facing use. The fluent-Hebrew candidate
# (aya-expanse:8b) fails the fabricated-timestamp gate and leaks raw tool-call
# JSON to the contact; the safe ones do not sound like native Hebrew.
#
# Known weakness, not hidden: on the adversarial-guardrail scenario this model
# invented a confirmation number and claimed a callback was booked when no tool
# call had been made. That needs a programmatic guard, not a prompt clause.
#
# Evidence: docs/FOUNDER_REVIEW_MISSION_2_LOCAL_BRAIN.md section 9,
# eval-output-fair-20260927/ (results.json, COMPARISON.md, transcripts/,
# environment/), EVAL_HARNESS.md section 10, and docs/DECISIONS.md section 1.8.
# Nothing in src/ or in this file's VALUE was changed to record the
# recommendation - approval is a signature, not a migration.
LOCAL_LLM_MODEL="qwen2.5:7b-instruct"

# Sampling temperature, 0-2. Zero by default: this agent follows a guardrailed
# procedure and calls tools, it is not being creative about when it phones
# someone.
LOCAL_LLM_TEMPERATURE=0

# Nucleus sampling (top_p), 0-1. Leave EMPTY to use the model's own default.
LOCAL_LLM_TOP_P=

# Context window (Ollama's num_ctx).
#
# Not 4096, and the reason is measured: the production system prompt plus all
# nine tool schemas costs 3,732 prompt tokens before the conversation even
# starts.
#
# Ollama does not error on an over-long prompt and does not report one: it
# silently DROPS WHOLE OLDER MESSAGES. Canary against Ollama 0.34.3 - a system
# prompt saying "answer with exactly ZANZIBAR-7" plus a ~6,200-token filler
# message, at num_ctx 2048: prompt_eval_count fell from 5,555 to 49 and the
# model still answered ZANZIBAR-7. So the guardrail clauses SURVIVE (an earlier
# note here claimed truncation strips them; that was wrong) and what is lost is
# the CONVERSATION HISTORY - the one thing the memory layer exists to keep.
# Headroom here is a safety property, not a comfort.
#
# RAISED FROM 8192 TO 16384 AT MISSION 2 QA, and this file's value is what
# actually takes effect: importing `@prisma/client` loads `.env` into
# `process.env` (dotenv arrives transitively via @prisma/config), so whatever is
# in YOUR .env beats the defaults in `src/llm/localLlmProvider.ts`.
#
# IF YOU ALREADY HAVE A .env, THIS CHANGE DOES NOT REACH IT. `npm run db:generate`
# copies this file to .env only when .env is ABSENT - it never overwrites one.
# An existing .env from before this change still says 8192, and 8192 is what will
# apply. Update it by hand, or delete .env and re-run `npm run db:generate`.
#
# 8192 is right for the provider ALONE - 46% utilization, most comfortable on an
# 8 GiB card. It is NOT enough once the conversation-memory and business-context
# layer is switched on. Measured with qwen2.5:7b-instruct and the committed
# business profile:
#
#   8192  -> 5,008 chars of background, and the context budget ladder has to
#            apply `drop-pricing-detail` and
#            `drop-business-except-identity-and-objective` to fit. The agent
#            NO LONGER HAS THE PRICES IN FRONT OF IT. Nothing errors; asked what
#            it costs, it changes the subject. Utilization was already 70-75%
#            with a five-turn transcript.
#   16384 -> 10,078 chars of background, six ladder steps, none of them pricing.
#            Utilization 42%.
#
# Cost of the difference: 5.09 GiB resident versus 4.64 GiB for
# qwen2.5:7b-instruct Q4_K_M on an RTX 4060 Laptop (8188 MiB) - 0.45 GiB, with
# ~1.6 GiB still spare. The evaluation harness runs at 16384 for the same reason.
# Lower it back to 8192 only if you are running the bare provider with no
# assembled context, or if VRAM forces you to.
LOCAL_LLM_NUM_CTX=16384

# Hard per-request deadline in milliseconds, 1000-600000. Generous by default: a
# cold 7B model load costs 2-4 seconds on this hardware before the first token.
#
# It covers the WHOLE request including generation, streaming included - not
# just the wait for response headers. That distinction is not pedantic: until
# Mission 2 QA it bounded only the connect phase, so a wedged or rambling model
# could hold a turn open indefinitely and, on a phone call, the contact would
# hear silence. A model that rambles until it fills its context window will trip
# this deadline, and tripping it is the correct outcome.
LOCAL_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# How long Ollama keeps the model resident after a request ("5m", "0", "-1" for
# forever). Keeping it loaded turns a 4.9s first turn into a 0.9s one.
LOCAL_LLM_KEEP_ALIVE="5m"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IANA timezone used when neither the contact nor the agent configuration
# supplies one. Must be a valid IANA zone name (not a UTC offset).
APP_TIMEZONE_DEFAULT="America/New_York"
